Read-only design consultation for the sefer-alloc Rust allocator. Do NOT edit any file, do NOT build, test, bench or run anything (no cargo, node, scripts, binaries). Allowed: reading files, grep/glob, read-only git (log/show/diff).

Trees: worktrees/g1-k5 (main + K1 + K5, current best) and worktrees/g1-p0 (branch g1-p0 = g1-k5 + commit 5fa16168: ceiling probe, class 3 = 64 B parks up to 1024 full magazines in a separate depot array, PerClass::parked u16, DEPOT_CAP u16). Background: worktrees/g1-pmu/docs/perf/G1_IDEAS.md §7–§8 and docs/perf/g1_pmu/c4/ (your previous answers: oh.md, rush_reviewer.md). Owner rules: protections against external input stay (double free, interior pointer, never-issued block, foreign pointer); no writes into the body of the block being freed inside dealloc (R1-01, item 164); everything must be measurable; only real wins against mimalloc on the J1′ judge are accepted; RSS retention is the owner's decision.

MEASURED (pre-registered P0, docs/perf/b4_ablate/G1_P0_PREREG.md on branch g1-p0-meas):
- J1′ v2 in-process ablation vs null (20 processes x 2000 blocks): bulk16k effect −56.13 % [−56.62, −54.74] (variant 1.75x faster), churn64 −5.90 % [−6.60, −5.37], bulk64 −2.76 % [−3.03, −2.52]; pair16, pair_rt, churn256, vec512, larson equal (CI has 0); pair64 +0.07 % [+0.02, +0.12]; churn16, churn1024 imprecise.
- Main judge vs mimalloc on g1-p0 (t_mimalloc/t_sefer, >1 = sefer faster): pair16 0.824, pair64 0.970, pair_rt 1.443, bulk64 0.815, bulk16k 0.873 (K1+K5 run: 0.511), churn16 0.637, churn64 0.804, churn256 0.743, churn1024 0.888 (not admitted by A/A), vec512 1.48 (not admitted), larson 1.029.
- Probe callgrind bulk16k: 85.7M -> 55.4M Ir, flush_class_internal and try_drain_freelist_batch gone from the top.
P0 parks up to 1,286,912 B of class-64 blocks per thread (pointer array 128 KiB per registry slot); it is not shippable as is.

Question: what is the best SHIPPABLE form that keeps most of this measured bulk16k win, and how to measure it so the verdict is real against mimalloc? Evaluate at least: (A) budgeted large depot (which classes, per-thread byte budget, a decay or trim policy, pointer storage that does not grow every registry slot by 128 KiB — e.g. lazily allocated chunked storage), with the RSS cost made explicit; (B) your earlier variant (d): FREE state byte + per-chunk summary bitmap as the free set (no body writes, no budget), refill by scanning; (C) anything better (e.g. making flush/drain themselves body-write-free so the transfer stays but gets cheap). For each: expected share of the P0 ceiling, RSS behaviour, invariants touched (state byte И1, double-free, remote frees / sidecar reclaim, segment reset/decommit, ghost rules in docs/verification/ghost-rules.json), implementation size, and the exact pre-registered judge thresholds you would require (including RSS measurement). Answer in Russian, concise, file:line references, MEASURED vs ESTIMATE separated. End with a ranked recommendation.
